Chapter 26 – Part 4
Curtis slipped the phone away and looked at Mika.
“Hey, Mika. Wanna come to the roof with me?” Long black braids shifted as he tipped his chin toward the ceiling. “I’m not allowed to smoke in the room. You can stay — but I don’t want you to think you can’t leave with me when I step out for a moment.”
Mika’s eyes flicked to the chained door, then to him.
The last boy who had stepped out of a room with her life in his hands had not always come back the same.
“…If I stay, I’ll sit here and wait for the handle to move.” Honest. “If I go, I get air and I get to see you don’t vanish into a cigarette.”
A short breath.
“I’ll come. Cap on. No street. Just roof.” She picked up the keycard and his number like talismans. “If someone else is up there, we turn around. I’m not doing another chase tonight.”
Curtis nodded.
“Roof. Smoke. Back. That’s the whole trip.”
He opened the door, waited for her to step into the hall first, then pulled it shut behind them.
The suite stayed locked on two beds and leftover cake.
The elevator climbed toward open sky —
and Mika Harima followed the boy who had promised she was not a problem, because staying behind while he stepped out still felt too much like being left.
The elevator hummed upward.
Curtis chuckled under his breath, long black braids shifting as he watched the floor numbers climb.
“Doubt mothmen come this far out.”
Mika turned her head so fast the white cap almost went crooked.
“Mothmen? You’re into fantasy books or something!”
Curtis’s mouth tugged. The forest, the real ones, the nights in Appalachia when the dark had wings and rules — none of that belonged in a hotel elevator with a girl who had already survived a lab.
He chuckled again, softer.
“Sure, Mika. Fantasy.”
She eyed him like she could hear the lie and had decided to let him keep it.
“…You’re a terrible liar when you smile like that.” Not pushing. Just tagging it. “Fine. Fantasy. If a mothman shows up on the roof I’m going back downstairs and billing your family.”
Curtis huffed.
“Deal.”
The elevator opened on a night that smelled like tar and city wind instead of trees.
On the roof of a high-roller hotel, a missing girl and a missing-poster boy stepped into open air —
and the mothmen, wherever they actually were, stayed in another country with the rest of the stories he was not telling tonight.
Curtis lit the cigarette against the wind and smirked at the skyline.
“Huh. I wonder what my parents would say if they found out I’m alive. Hahaha.” Smoke pulled away over Ikebukuro. Long black braids shifted. “You sending them a bill would be priceless. I would pay to see the look on their faces.”
Mika stared at him.
The laugh was real. The joke was not kind. It had a forest in it, and a missing poster, and a five-year-old who had not been wanted enough to keep.
“…That’s a messed up punchline,” she said, not scolding so much as clocking it. “You’re talking like them finding out you’re alive would be the embarrassing part.”
A short breath.
“If they’re the reason you know how to disappear, then yeah. I’d bill them. For the suite. For the cake. For the fact you think mothmen and dead parents are the same flavor of joke.” She glanced at him sideways. “You don’t have to tell me the story. Just… if you ever do send that bill, I want to sign it.”
Curtis’s smile thinned into something almost honest.
“Yeah. That’d be worth the postage.”
He took another drag and let the city hold the rest.
On the hotel roof, two people who had been erased in different ways shared a cigarette break —
and Mika Harima filed the laugh away as proof that some families only got funnier after you survived them.
Curtis took a drag.
“I’m not going to sugar coat it.”
He lifted his phone, thumbed to a saved image he should not have still carried, and turned the screen toward her.
A missing kid poster.
Five years old. Northwest Territories. A face that was his if you knew how to look — darker, smaller, braids not yet a choice so much as what someone else had put in his hair. MISSING. A date that had lived longer than the boy in the photograph.
Wind moved over the roof. The cigarette burned in his other hand.
Mika went still.
The white cap brim hid her eyes for a second, then she looked again, because looking away would have been worse.
“…That’s you.” Not a question. “Five. They put you on paper and the paper never got to take you down.”
A short, thin breath.
“You weren’t kidding about the missing-poster kid.” She stared at the Northwest Territories line like geography could explain a disappearance. “That’s not a rumor. That’s a whole country that was supposed to look for you.”
She did not reach for the phone. She did not ask about the forest yet.
“You showed me that like it’s just another label on a bottle.” Her voice dropped. “It’s not. It’s a kid. It’s you. And you’re standing here smoking on a roof in Japan like the poster lost and you didn’t.”
Curtis took the phone back, locked it, and exhaled smoke toward a city that had never seen that picture on a lamppost.
“Yeah,” he said. “Poster lost.”
On the hotel roof, Mika Harima understood that the boy who kept calling her a person had once been a face on a flyer —
and that he had shown her the ugly version on purpose, the same way he refused to let her live as only Celty.
Curtis watched the smoke thin.
“I was never found because they filed me missing in the Northwest Territories, where we lived.” Long black braids shifted in the roof wind. “But they abandoned me on purpose in Appalachia. So nobody ever came to find me.”
The words sat on the skyline like a closed case that had been built to stay closed.
He flicked the cigarette over the edge, ember dying on the way down.
“Let’s go, Mika. You will catch a cold out here.”
Mika looked at him for a second longer than the cold required.
Two maps. One lie. A child placed on the wrong side of a country so the search would run in circles until it got tired.
“…That’s not disappearing,” she said quietly. “That’s being hidden by the people who were supposed to keep you.”
She still followed when he turned toward the stairwell, arms folded against the wind, white cap pulled down.
“Okay. Inside. I’m not arguing with a guy who just showed me his own missing poster and then worried about my cold.” A thinner beat, almost dry. “You’re a lot of things, Curtis. Subtle isn’t one of them.”
He held the door.
On the way back down to the fifth floor, Ikebukuro’s heat met them like a lesser forest —
and Mika Harima walked beside a boy the official world had been told to look for in the wrong place,
while the right place had already taught him how to survive without being found.
Back in the suite, the chain went on again.
Mika took the far bed without being told. Curtis stayed near the bar, phone already in his hand, Anri’s trust sitting heavier than the number she had sent.
He opened a new thread.
