Curtis locked the phone and let his head tip back against the bar.
Morning. An address. A classmate who wanted to be a hero and had still given him a door that was not a lab.
On the fifth floor, the suite had an ending time.
Mika Harima did not know it yet.
Ikebukuro, for a few more hours, would have to wait.
Mikado sat on the edge of his bed with the address sent and the jealousy still there, ugly and unhelpful.
He was jealous of Curtis.
Of the height. The ease. The way Anri said Curtis-kun like the room had already chosen a center. Of Rio standing at that other shoulder. Of a foreigner who slept through class and still somehow became the person people crashed into when they needed saving.
And if he was honest — which the texts had forced him to be — Curtis had never actually done anything intentional to one-up him.
Not the introductions.
Not the class-rep accident.
Not even the shoes.
The new pair had been care, not a performance aimed at Mikado’s ruined old ones. Curtis had not stood in the doorway to win. He had stood there because Anri’s soles were coming apart. Mikado had walked in on kindness and decided it was a ranking.
Now that same boy was coming to him for help.
Not Anri first as a prize. Not Masaomi as the city-smart one. Mikado Ryuugamine. Address in the morning. Mika in tow. An apology Mikado had not earned as cleanly as he wanted.
The jealousy did not vanish. It just had nowhere honest to stand.
Curtis Nightstar had not stolen the current.
He had been walking through it, half-asleep, buying shoes and catching falling girls, while Mikado watched and kept score.
Tomorrow morning, the score would not matter.
A missing girl would be at his door because the person he resented had trusted him anyway.
Mikado put the phone face-down and stared at the dark.
He would help.
He would still feel the sting.
And for the first time since Raira started, he wondered whether being relevant in Anri’s eyes was a smaller thing than being the kind of guy Curtis had just decided he could trust.
Mikado spoke it to the empty room, quiet enough that even the walls could pretend they had not heard.
“I may need the Dollars.”
The next sentence cost more.
“And I may have to step into the light and reveal myself.”
Leader of a crowd that was not supposed to have a face. TarouTanaka in a chat. A small-town kid who had built a current and then stood on the bank acting like he was only visiting. If Yagiri was in this, if Shizuo was in this, if the Black Rider was in this — a classmate’s hotel and one boy’s good intentions would not be enough to move a reconstructed girl across Ikebukuro.
The Dollars could make space.
A rumor. A corridor. Bodies that were not Masaomi’s, not Anri’s, not Curtis standing alone in a fancy doorway.
To use them properly, someone would have to admit they had a hand on the switch.
Mikado’s hands were cold.
Curtis had asked can I trust you and meant a good guy with an address.
He had not asked the founder of the Dollars.
If Mikado stepped forward, Anri would see a different boy. Masaomi would hear the thing he had been trying to keep out of their lunch table. The city would finally have a name to pin on a color that was supposed to stay fog.
He picked the phone back up, then set it down without typing.
Morning first.
Curtis. Mika. A door.
If the street between the hotel and his building turned into a hunt, then — maybe — the leader would have to stop hiding behind a handle.
Mikado Ryuugamine sat in the dark and felt the current under his feet.
Helping a missing girl might be the moment he stopped being only a classmate.
It might also be the moment Ikebukuro found out who had been running the rumor all along.
